  "generated_text": "To find the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that all the roots of the equation \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( n \\)-th roots of unity, we need to analyze the roots of the given polynomial.\n\nFirst, let's solve the equation \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\). We can make a substitution to simplify this equation. Let \\( w = z^2 \\). Then the equation becomes:\n\\[\nw^2 + w + 1 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in \\( w \\). We solve it using the quadratic formula \\( w = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 1 \\), and \\( c = 1 \\):\n\\[\nw = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1}}{2 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 - 4}}{2} = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2} = \\frac{-1 \\pm i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the solutions for \\( w \\) are:\n\\[\nw = \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad w = \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the corresponding values of \\( z \\). Recall that \\( w = z^2 \\), so we solve for \\( z \\):\n\\[\nz^2 = \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad z^2 = \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThe numbers \\( \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\) and \\( \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\) are the non-real cube roots of unity, denoted by \\( \\omega \\) and \\( \\omega^2 \\), respectively, where \\( \\omega = e^{2\\pi i / 3} \\) and \\( \\omega^2 = e^{-2\\pi i / 3} \\). These roots satisfy:\n\\[\n\\omega^3 = 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad (\\omega^2)^3 = 1\n\\]\n\nThus, the solutions for \\( z \\) are:\n\\[\nz = \\pm \\sqrt{\\omega} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad z = \\pm \\sqrt{\\omega^2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( \\omega \\) and \\( \\omega^2 \\) are roots of unity, we need to determine the smallest \\( n \\) such that \\( \\sqrt{\\omega} \\) and \\( \\sqrt{\\omega^2} \\) are \\( n \\)-th roots of unity. Note that:\n\\[\n\\omega = e^{2\\pi i / 3} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\omega^2 = e^{-2\\pi i / 3}\n\\]\n\nThe magnitude of \\( \\omega \\) and \\( \\omeg